  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nAn individual E8 decoder term can cost six after 25 punctures when its normals are parallel roots. This package proves that every exact eight-term completion containing it costs at least **67 copies**, and supplies an attaining completion. The minimum at 25 punctures over all exact eight-term complex decoders is **56**, also attained. Thus the copy-cost floor extends from `p<=24` to `p<=25`; the first dimension-seven support does not improve the complete copy invoice.\n\nFor arbitrary **nonparallel complex normals** with nonzero ordinary bilinear product, cost six first occurs at 26 punctures. This extends #1020's root-pair lower bound and reuses its credited attaining witness. No new kappa, physical supplier or selected-frontier change is claimed.\n\nThe written proof inherits #968's real E8 root-hyperplane classification F6. The finite replays establish its listed premises and examples; they are not a proof-assistant theorem or independent human review. Physical source/helper/frame/restoration costs, all-size/compiler realization, hosting, precision/tape, bit interfaces and analytic transfer remain outside this result.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\nBase: `ef5f39403610d231a0c27e8a6b4f08c10209bcd6`. Contribution commit: `900658976e42a1501b3d23d46b2ea3cab228bc4c`. OS assignment seed **31**, complex-supplier track. Only `research/complex-general-normal-boundary/` is added.\n\nThe scope is explicitly **changed** from the catalogue's `complex-masked-decoder` card to a deeper-puncture all-pairs boundary result. `TASK.json` records three candidates, the complete domain, stopping rule and local claim; no pinned coordination issue existed. The optimistic copy/port target fails before physical synthesis: `67/95 > 56/95 > 7/13`.\n\nThe immediate pre-submission refresh covered 765 open PRs and 198 discussion records with complete pagination and no retrieval warnings; no competing result in this scope was identified.\n\nNearest work: #968/#1077 stop at 24 punctures; #1020 (`bf4ee95df4ee6e3101a88f5d015c8aa85b688b6d`) excludes only distinct nonorthogonal root normals through 25; #1180 extends factor-linear term counts only through 24. #1187/#1455 concern a prescribed P104 decoder at a different boundary. Exact source Git objects and SHA256 pins are in `SOURCES.json`; pending overlaps are not theorem premises.\n\n## Validation\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/complex-general-normal-boundary\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/complex-general-normal-boundary outcome=negative-result`.\n\n- Complete census of **7,560** boundary punctures, **63** per root, with verified symmetry transport.\n- All **32,193** rational matrices checked by the primary integer-elimination replay and a separately written integer Gram-minor checker that imports no producer or predecessor code.\n- Two complete 95-port decoder examples checked on all **18,050** live ordered pairs; all representative 67-copy completions checked.\n- Eight primary controls and eight scoped tests reject wrong scale, undercharged bills, omitted cuts/functionals, false kernels/ranks and source corruption.\n- Registered Python **3.12.2** replay passed on macOS arm64. Both checkers passed in an isolated read-only relocation under optimized Python **3.14.6**. Source pins, selected-record integrity, Ruff and staged diff checks passed. `VALIDATION.json` binds the checked core files.\n\nNo full selected cold replay or native/hosted supplier admission was run. Hosted CI is separate from these local results.\n\n## Credit\n\nCredit DreamingOfClouds (#717), themilkman216 (#968), woklerx (#1077/#1020), and the retained Rohan Arun/Jeremy Kothe/Hao Yan/bit-supplier lineage. The frame-pair attaining mechanism and 26-puncture witness retain predecessor credit.\n\nPrepared by **Hanson Sun (@bbMisk)** with substantial **OpenAI Codex assistance** in research, proof, both implementations, verification and writing. Apache-2.0; frozen predecessor sources and `upstream/` are unchanged.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nThe package README and `TASK.json` describe the unresolved complete decoder minimum at 26 punctures and a possible self-contained replacement for the cited F6 classification. Their first steps and blockers are explicit; neither is claimed completed here.\n",
